This week is about practice more than new ideas. Recursion is the main way functional programs repeat things, and the aim is for writing recursive functions to become second nature. That only comes from writing many of them. So these notes contain many small worked examples, all following the same recipe, and the drills and lab contain many more. Do as many as you can.

1.1 A definition in terms of itself
The factorial of a natural number n is the product 1 \times 2 \times \cdots \times n, written n!. The “\cdots” is fine on paper, but a program needs something more precise. Notice that

5! = 5 \times \underbrace{4 \times 3 \times 2 \times 1}_{4!} = 5 \times 4!

In general, n! = n \times (n-1)!. The factorial of n is defined in terms of the factorial of a smaller number. Together with a starting point, this gives a complete definition:

n! =
\begin{cases}
1 & \text{if } n = 0 \\
n \times (n-1)! & \text{if } n &gt; 0
\end{cases}

(By convention 0! = 1, the empty product.) This translates directly into F#:
let rec factIf n =
    if n = 0 then 1
    else n * factIf (n - 1)
A function that calls itself is recursive. In F# you must say so with the keyword rec. Without it, the name factIf isn’t bound yet inside its own body:
&gt; let fact n = if n = 0 then 1 else n * fact (n - 1);;

  let fact n = if n = 0 then 1 else n * fact (n - 1);;
  --------------------------------------^^^^

error FS0039: The value or constructor 'fact' is not defined.

6.2 Generic types
Some functions don’t care what type their argument has:
let identity x = x
let first x y = x
let alwaysZero _ = 0
&gt; let identity x = x;;
val identity: x: 'a -&gt; 'a

&gt; let first x y = x;;
val first: x: 'a -&gt; y: 'b -&gt; 'a

&gt; let alwaysZero _ = 0;;
val alwaysZero: 'a -&gt; int
A name starting with an apostrophe, like 'a (read “alpha”, or just “a”), is a type variable. It stands for any type. identity: 'a -&gt; 'a works for int, string, or anything else, and the type promises that the result has the same type as the argument. first: 'a -&gt; 'b -&gt; 'a takes two arguments of possibly different types and returns something of the first one’s type. Such functions are called generic (the technical term is parametric polymorphism).
A generic type says a lot about what a function can do. A function of type 'a -&gt; 'a knows nothing about its argument: it can’t add to it, print it, or compare it. So all it can do is hand it back. The type alone tells you what identity does.
Parentheses in a type mark a parameter that is itself a function:
let twice f x = f (f x)
let plus2 = twice (add 1)
&gt; let twice f x = f (f x);;
val twice: f: ('a -&gt; 'a) -&gt; x: 'a -&gt; 'a
twice takes a function f (of type 'a -&gt; 'a) and a value x, and applies f two times. twice (add 1) is a function that adds 2. Functions that take or return functions are called higher-order functions; they are the subject of Week 5.

